 "procedure": {
  "source": "raw/certicom/cert_ecc_challenge.pdf (1997 doc, wayback 2009 copy identical size) and challenge-2009.pdf, Sec 3.1.4 / 3.2.4",
  "hash": "SHA-1 (FIPS 180-1); seed = arbitrary 160-bit string, \"Choose an arbitrary bit string seedE of length 160 bits\"",
  "prime_Fp": "t=log2 p, s=(t-1)//160, h=t-160s. W0 = h rightmost bits of SHA-1(seed) with leftmost bit cleared; Wi=SHA-1((seed+i) mod 2^160), i=1..s; r = W0||W1||...||Ws; choose a,b with r*b^2 = a^3 mod p; reject if 4a^3+27b^2=0",
  "binary_F2m": "s=(m-1)//160, h=m-160s. b0 = h rightmost bits of SHA-1(seed); bi=SHA-1((seed+i) mod 2^160); b=b0||...||bs; a arbitrary (0/1 etc.)",
  "points": "Same hash-expansion applied to seedP -> x-coordinate xU (top bit cleared in prime case); must lie on curve; P = h*U; Q (public key) likewise from seedQ so private key unknown to Certicom. Doc notes a typo: Alg 3/7 step 1 says seedE but means seedP.",
  "koblitz": "ECC2K curves: no seedE (a,b fixed); points still from seedP/seedQ.",
  "doc_says_nothing_about": "how the 160-bit seeds were chosen beyond \"arbitrary\"; no mention of MinghuaQu.",
  "verification": "certicom_verification.json: all 66 seed->(b|r*b^2=a^3 | U_x | V_x | P_x) checks pass with SHA-1; every one of the 66 seeds contains ASCII MinghuaQu at a nibble-aligned bit offset."
 }
